WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.380
This video shows how to classify sequencing reads with TaxTriage inside Lungfish Genome Explorer, and how to judge what it finds.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:16.525
The example is one air sample from an airport surveillance study.

00:00:16.525 --> 00:00:26.287
Choose File, then Search Online Databases, then Search SRA, and enter the run accession SRR35520577.

00:00:26.287 --> 00:00:32.340
LGE looks up the run and shows its instrument, title and size.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.478
Select the run and click Download Selected.

00:00:36.478 --> 00:00:41.988
The Import FASTQ sheet reads the platform and pairing from the archive record.

00:00:41.988 --> 00:00:50.395
The VSP2 Target Enrichment recipe removes duplicates, trims adapters, removes human reads and merges overlapping pairs.

00:00:50.395 --> 00:00:56.400
These reads are already in the project, imported with this recipe, so we cancel here.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.027
Reads on your own computer come in the same way.

00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:10.066
Choose File, then Import Center, and under Sequencing Read Files click Import to pick FASTQ files from a folder or a drive.

00:01:10.066 --> 00:01:13.740
A sample sheet or a Nanopore run folder works too.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.986
Search the sidebar for the run and select it.

00:01:17.986 --> 00:01:29.800
The cards show about 254 thousand reads after cleanup, and the Inspector on the right lists the archive metadata, such as the study, the sample and the capture strategy.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:34.043
Under Classification, choose TaxTriage.

00:01:34.043 --> 00:01:37.751
LGE checks that Nextflow and Docker are ready.

00:01:37.751 --> 00:01:43.071
Pick the Viral Kraken2 database and keep Illumina as the platform.

00:01:43.071 --> 00:01:51.534
Skip assembly keeps the run fast, and human reads, taxon 9606, are excluded before references are chosen.

00:01:51.534 --> 00:01:52.340
Click Run.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.823
The run appears in the Operations panel with its current step and elapsed time.

00:01:58.823 --> 00:02:03.524
Click Log to see the exact command and each pipeline step as it runs.

00:02:03.524 --> 00:02:06.727
This sample took about two minutes on a laptop.

00:02:06.727 --> 00:02:11.360
When it finishes, the result is saved under Analyses in the project.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:21.993
Each row is an organism, ranked by its TASS score, a confidence value from zero to one based on how the reads align.

00:02:21.993 --> 00:02:25.680
The Summary tab records the database and settings.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:31.170
SARS-CoV-2 has 575 reads, but only 2 unique reads.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:41.180
The viewer draws coverage across its genome, and nearly all of it sits in one spot, about 1 percent of the genome.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:47.480
Tick Include duplicate-marked reads, and the depth jumps to 573.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:53.900
Up close, every read starts at the same position, copies of very few molecules.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:57.800
Colored bases only mean no reference was loaded.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:04.193
Compare phiX174, the control DNA commonly spiked into Illumina runs.

00:03:04.193 --> 00:03:10.731
Its 8 reads are all unique and spread across the genome, which is why it scores 0.9.

00:03:10.731 --> 00:03:16.180
Here it confirms the sequencing worked, not that phage was in the air.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.553
A high read count alone is not a detection.

00:03:20.553 --> 00:03:27.960
To check a hit, send its reads to BLAST, or take the reads out of LGE and test them with other tools.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:33.298
Select the SARS-CoV-2 row and click BLAST Verify.

00:03:33.298 --> 00:03:44.803
LGE sends a sample of its reads, 20 by default, to NCBI BLAST against the core nucleotide database, and tracks the job while it runs.

00:03:44.803 --> 00:03:53.540
Here, 2 reads match SARS-CoV-2 at 99.5 and 100 percent identity, and 2 reads find no significant hit.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:56.740
Expand a read to see its top matches.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:01.776
To use the reads elsewhere, click Extract FASTQ.

00:04:01.776 --> 00:04:13.077
Choose FASTQ or FASTA, then save them as a bundle in the project, save a file, copy them to the clipboard to paste into a web tool, or share them.

00:04:13.077 --> 00:04:14.780
Here we save a bundle.

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:23.660
The new bundle appears under Extractions with its 2 reads, ready for any other LGE tool or for export.

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:27.120
The database sets the question.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:32.981
Viral holds only RefSeq viral genomes and suits a virus screen like this one.

00:04:32.981 --> 00:04:38.614
Standard-16 adds bacteria, archaea and human, for a broad pathogen screen.

00:04:38.614 --> 00:04:45.160
SILVA holds ribosomal RNA genes, for asking which bacteria and eukaryotes are present.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:56.750
TaxTriage is built and maintained by a team at the Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory, and we thank them for it.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:05:06.020
The paper is in the journal Bioinformatics, and the code and documentation are at github.com/jhuapl-bio/taxtriage.
